#==============================================================================================
#
#   engine - The column interpreter behind a Code node
#
#   DESCRIPTION:
#       A program is a flat list of instructions over typed column registers: each
#       instruction runs over a chunk of lanes (4096 by default) at once, as a tight loop
#       over slices, the way Houdini runs VEX. `Builder` makes a program; `run` binds the
#       host's named columns, runs the chunks on a thread pool and writes the named output
#       columns. One lane is one element (a point, a face, a pixel): the engine knows
#       nothing about what the lanes are.
#
#       Failure: a program that would trap in Base (bounds, overflow, division by zero, a
#       failed conversion, assert, trap, a runaway loop) stops the run with a `Fault`
#       naming the source line and column and the lowest failing lane, the same at any
#       thread count. The engine itself never traps: every index it forms is proven by the
#       chunk bounds and by `verify`, which every program passes before it can run.
#
#==============================================================================================
